---
// Multiple testing vs. the winner's curse, on the page's own seeded dashboards. Every number below
// is computed at build time by measureWinnersCurse (src/lib/stats-sim/winners-curse.ts) and pinned
// in tests/stats-sim/winners-curse.test.ts, nothing is typed in by hand. Astro drops a line break
// that touches a tag or an {expression}, so the prose below only breaks lines between plain words.
import { measureWinnersCurse, type Rule } from "../../../lib/stats-sim/winners-curse";

const result = measureWinnersCurse();
const { config } = result;

const count = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString("en-US");
const lift = (x: number) => `${x >= 0 ? "+" : "−"}${Math.round(Math.abs(x) * 100)}%`;
const share = (x: number) => `${(x * 100).toFixed(1)}%`;
const points = (x: number) => (x * 100).toFixed(2);

const liftedPerDashboard = config.num_metrics - Math.round(config.num_metrics * config.fraction_null);
const labels: Record<Rule, string> = {
	none: "Flagged, no correction",
	bh: "Flagged, Benjamini-Hochberg",
	bonferroni: "Flagged, Bonferroni",
};
const rows = (["none", "bh", "bonferroni"] as const).map((rule) => ({ rule, label: labels[rule], ...result.rules[rule] }));
const { none, bh, bonferroni } = result.rules;
---

<div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 md:px-12">
	<aside id="winners-curse" class="raw-box scroll-mt-24 rounded-xl bg-cream p-6" aria-labelledby="winners-curse-heading">
		<p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-accent-blue">An aside: this isn't the winner's curse</p>
		<h3 id="winners-curse-heading" class="mt-2 font-display text-lg font-semibold">Is a flagged winner real? How big is it? Two different questions.</h3>
		<p class="mt-2 max-w-[65ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
			Calling something a "winner" invites a mix-up with a neighboring idea. Multiple testing, this page's problem,
			asks whether a flagged winner is real at all. The <strong class="text-ink">winner's curse</strong> asks how big
			a real winner is: the metrics that clear the bar are, by construction, the ones whose noise happened to push them
			up, so their measured lift overstates their true one. Same flags, two different ways to be wrong, and the
			corrections above only fix the first.
		</p>

		<div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto">
			<table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
				<thead>
					<tr class="border-b border-line align-bottom font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-soft">
						<th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-3 md:pr-4 font-normal">Which metrics</th>
						<th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-3 md:pr-4 font-normal">
							<span class="block text-accent-blue">Is it real?</span>
							Flags on a null metric
						</th>
						<th scope="col" class="py-2 font-normal">
							<span class="block text-accent-blue">How big is it?</span>
							Average observed lift
						</th>
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr class="border-b border-line/60 align-top">
						<th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-3 md:pr-4 font-normal text-ink-soft">Every real lift, flagged or not</th>
						<td class="py-2 pr-3 md:pr-4 font-mono text-ink-soft">&mdash;</td>
						<td class="py-2 font-mono">
							{lift(result.mean_observed_lift_all)}
							<span class="block text-xs text-ink-soft">{count(result.lifted_metrics)} metrics</span>
						</td>
					</tr>
					{
						rows.map((row) => (
							<tr class="border-b border-line/60 align-top">
								<th scope="row" class="py-2 pr-3 md:pr-4 font-normal text-ink">{row.label}</th>
								<td class="py-2 pr-3 md:pr-4 font-mono">
									{share(row.null_share)}
									<span class="block text-xs text-ink-soft">
										{count(row.null_flags)} of {count(row.flags)} flags
									</span>
								</td>
								<td class="py-2 font-mono">
									{lift(row.mean_observed_lift)}
									<span class="block text-xs text-ink-soft">{count(row.lifted_flags)} real winners</span>
								</td>
							</tr>
						))
					}
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>

		<p class="mt-5 max-w-[65ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
			Read down the columns. Each stricter rule cuts the fake flags, from {share(none.null_share)} to {share(bh.null_share)} to {share(bonferroni.null_share)}, and
			pushes the real winners' average further from the truth, from {lift(none.mean_observed_lift)} to {lift(bh.mean_observed_lift)} to {lift(bonferroni.mean_observed_lift)}, on a
			true lift of {lift(result.true_lift)}. A higher bar fixes false discoveries and makes the curse worse, the only
			real effects that clear it are the ones that drew the luckiest noise. So a correction tells you which winners to
			believe, not how big they are. To size one, re-test it on fresh traffic, where nothing got selected.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-xs leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
			{count(result.dashboards)} simulated dashboards, seeds {count(result.first_seed)}–{count(result.last_seed)}, at the
			live experiment's defaults: {config.num_metrics} metrics, {liftedPerDashboard} of them lifted by a true {lift(result.true_lift)} on a {Math.round(config.baseline_rate * 100)}% baseline, {count(config.visitors_per_arm)} visitors per
			arm, α = {result.alpha}. The yardstick is {lift(result.mean_observed_lift_all)}, not {lift(result.true_lift)}, and
			that first gap isn't the curse: a ratio of two noisy rates runs high on average even when nothing is selected.
			Measured as a plain difference in rates, which doesn't, every lifted metric averages {points(result.mean_observed_diff_all)} percentage
			points against a true {points(result.true_diff)}. Computed at build time by src/lib/stats-sim/winners-curse.ts and
			pinned in tests/stats-sim/winners-curse.test.ts.
		</p>
	</aside>
</div>
